## Getting started

Create your environment file first (the app reads secrets from it):

```bash
cp .env.example .env
# Windows PowerShell: Copy-Item .env.example .env
```

Then edit `.env`:

- Set `SECRET_KEY` to a random value of at least 50 characters. Generate one
  with:

  ```bash
  python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(50))"
  ```

{%- if database == 'postgresql' %}

- Set `POSTGRES_PASSWORD`. Do this **before** the first Docker start, because
  PostgreSQL only applies it when the volume is initialized.
{%- endif %}

### Docker-only development (recommended)

No local Python installation is required. The development image installs
`requirements-dev.txt` and runs Django's autoreloading server:

```bash
docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f compose.dev.yml up --build
```

The app is available at <http://localhost:{{ port }}>. The source tree is bind
mounted, so changes reload automatically. The overlay forces `DEBUG=True`,
connects to the Docker database when using PostgreSQL, and uses `runserver`.

If you have GNU make installed, `make dev` is the same command.

Run tests and linting inside the development container:

```bash
docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f compose.dev.yml run --rm web pytest
docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f compose.dev.yml run --rm web ruff check .
docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f compose.dev.yml run --rm web black --check .
```

The equivalent make targets are `make dev-test` and `make dev-lint`.

### Production-like Docker run

```bash
docker compose up --build
```

This runs Gunicorn with `DEBUG=False`. The app will be available at
<http://localhost:{{ port }}>.

Static files are collected during the image build. Rebuild the image after
changing static files:

```bash
docker compose up --build
```

### Local development with venv (optional)

You do not need a local Python environment when using the Docker-only workflow.
If you prefer a local venv:

```bash
python -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate   # Windows: .venv\Scripts\activate
pip install -r requirements-dev.txt
{%- if include_precommit %}
pre-commit install
{%- endif %}

python backend/manage.py migrate
python backend/manage.py runserver
```

{%- if database == 'postgresql' %}

`.env.example` uses `DEBUG=True` and `DB_HOST=localhost`, so PostgreSQL works
with a local server. Docker Compose overrides `DEBUG=False` and `DB_HOST=db`.
{%- else %}

`.env.example` uses `DEBUG=True` for local development. Docker Compose
overrides it to `DEBUG=False`.
{%- endif %}

Create an admin user:

```bash
# Docker-only workflow
docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f compose.dev.yml run --rm web \
  python backend/manage.py createsuperuser

# Local venv
python backend/manage.py createsuperuser
```

Then open <http://localhost:{{ port }}/admin/>.

## Make targets

If you have GNU make installed (Git Bash or WSL on Windows):

```bash
make help        # list all targets
make dev         # Docker-only development stack (foreground)
make dev-down    # stop the development stack
make dev-test    # pytest inside the development container
make dev-lint    # ruff + black inside the development container
make dev-shell   # Django shell inside the development container
make up          # production-like Docker stack in the background
make down        # stop the production-like stack
make migrate     # apply migrations with the local venv
make superuser   # create an admin user with the local venv
make test        # run pytest in the local venv
make lint        # ruff + black check in the local venv
make fmt         # auto-fix with the local venv
```

